  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 144 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nSunday starts with northwest flow aloft, eventually transitioning to \nmore zonal flow by Sunday night. Some lingering flurries are \npossible across portions of north central Nebraska as the surface \nlow continues to track east, but otherwise, little is expected in \nterms of impactful weather. In fact, this pattern will support a bit \nof a warming pattern heading into early next week. Highs are \nexpected to steadily climb from the mid 40s on Sunday to the mid 50s \nby Tuesday and Wednesday. While the upper level pattern will support \na few surface systems tracking across the northern and southern \nPlains, these are largely expected to miss the region, keeping \nprecipitation chances limited through mid week. \n\nWednesday night into Thursday, a deep upper level low tracks across \nthe southern Plains, supporting a surface low pressure system across \nthe central Plains. This system represents our next best chances for \nprecipitation, mainly areas across southwest into north central \nNebraska. While the low is currently forecast to remain well south \nof the region, wrap around precipitation on the northeast quadrant \nof the low may still clip portions of the region, bringing another \nchance for rainfall. There is still some subtle differences in \ntiming and location between the longer term models, but generally, \nthe GFS and ECMWF are tracking the same system in the same general \nlocation. Ensembles generally keep chances for precipitation to \nexceed one tenth of an inch around 20 to 40 percent. Given some \npotential for overnight lows to drop below freezing again, cannot \ncompletely rule out the potential for some snow showers with this \nsystem. However, given the subtle differences in the models, \nconfidence in impacts with this system remains relatively low. Will \ncontinue to keep a close eye on forecast trends through the weekend \nto see if these subtle differences ultimately converge on a \nsolution.",
